NAME
    chown - change a file's owner and/or group

SYNOPSIS
    chown <user|user:group|:group> <file>

DESCRIPTION
    Sets the owning user and/or group of <file>. Each field is either a NAME,
    resolved through /etc/passwd (users) and /etc/group (groups), or a NUMERIC
    id. Forms:

        user         set the user, leave the group
        user:group   set both
        :group       set the group, leave the user
        user:        set the user, leave the group (same as user)

    A field made entirely of digits is taken as an id and is never looked up as
    a name - the same rule su and useradd -g follow, so "chown 1000 f" always
    means uid 1000 even if some account is literally named "1000".

    An unknown name is an error ("no such user: bob"); nothing is changed.

    DIVERGENCE, on purpose: in POSIX/GNU chown a trailing colon ("chown alice:
    f") also sets the group to alice's login group. Here it leaves the group
    alone, matching the numeric form's behaviour. Write "chown alice:alice" to
    set both.

    ext2 ONLY. Ownership is an ext2 concept; on FAT32, exFAT, or /proc chown
    reports "not supported by this filesystem". ls -l shows the numeric owner
    on ext2 and a dash elsewhere.

    ROOT ONLY (enforced by the filesystem server): changing a file's owner is
    exactly the operation that could give a file away, so a non-root caller is
    refused with "permission denied".

EXAMPLES
    chown root file            set owner to root
    chown alice:staff file     set owner alice, group staff
    chown :staff file          set group staff, leave the owner
    chown 0 file               set owner to uid 0 (numeric still works)
    chown 501:20 file          set owner 501, group 20

SEE ALSO
    chmod, ls, id, passwd, stat
